## PHASE 2 — Build the fixes into the visual system

The interface currently reads as unfinished: near-black, one green, flat
surfaces, no depth. Pons looks more finished because of spacing, contrast
hierarchy, and card elevation — not because it is more colourful.

We already have a stronger visual language available and are not using it. The
brand assets introduced bone/marble, the Greek meander, and serif capitals.
Bring all three into the product so the X profile, the marketing site and the
app finally look like one thing.

### Palette — extend, don't brighten

```
Ground            #0A0C0B
Surface           #101412
Surface raised    #161B18      elevated cards, the preview panel
Surface hover     #1A211C
Border            #1C211E
Border strong     #2E352F
Border accent     #16301A      on cards carrying backing data

Accent (patina)   #00C805
Accent deep       #0E2A12
Accent mid        #2E6B33

Bone              #DDD8CA      headings, brand moments, primary emphasis
Bone muted        #8A938D
Faint             #5F665F

Positive          #00C805
Negative          #FF5A52
Warning           #EF9F27
Warning surface   #1A1509

Chart series      #00C805  #2E6B33  #DDD8CA  #6E8B77  #C9A96A
```

Bone as the heading tone is the single biggest change. It stops the interface
reading as monochrome without adding a decorative colour. Green stays strictly
functional — price direction, backing, confirmation.

### Typography

Two registers, consistently applied:

- **Serif** (from the brand assets): page titles, token names on the token
  page, section headings, the numbers in the analytics hero. Letterspaced,
  bone-toned.
- **Sans**: body, labels, form controls, table data, anything dense.

Loosen the header wordmark from `tracking-tight` to about `tracking-[0.15em]`
so it matches the brand lockup.

Numbers use tabular figures everywhere so columns align and values do not jitter
when they update.

### Depth and spacing

- Cards get `Surface raised` plus a 1px border, not a shadow. Shadows read as
  cheap on near-black.
- Cards carrying backing data get `Border accent` — this makes the panel that
  matters visually distinct without a badge.
- Increase vertical rhythm. The current layout is cramped; Pons breathes more.
  Section spacing ~48px desktop, ~32px mobile.
- The create-page preview card is **sticky** on desktop and stays in view while
  the form scrolls.

### Meander

Use the Greek meander pattern from the brand assets as a section rule: beneath
page titles, above the footer, as the divider inside the backing panel. Two per
screen maximum. This is what visually ties app, marketing and social together,
and no competitor has anything like it.

